\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two short arcs merge into the opposite edge}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Take the three points #{0}, #{1/2} and #{3/4} in the
[dyadic index](fdip-00B6). The successive arcs are #{1/2},
#{1/4} and #{1/4}. The point #{3/4} lies between the two arcs
of length #{1/4}. \ref{fdip-00B8} replaces those two arcs by
one arc of length #{1/2}, with coupling
#{-m/\sinh(m/2)}. The edge that was already present, from
#{0} to #{1/2}, has the same coupling. The two parallel
couplings add, so the off-diagonal entry of the retained
precision is #{-2m/\sinh(m/2)}. Each diagonal entry receives
#{m\coth(m/2)} from the merged edge and #{m\coth(m/2)} from
the kept edge, hence equals #{2m\coth(m/2)}. This is the
two-point [cyclic arc precision](fdip-00B6) for the arc
#{1/2}.}
